  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nUpper high over the Four Corners will lead to dry weather locally\nover the weekend. Surface high spanning across the High Plains \nwill maintain east flow and a subtle cool air advection pattern \nacross Southern New Mexico and Far West Texas. This will keep \ntemperatures slightly below normal for the start of October, with \nlowland highs only reaching the mid-to-upper 70s each afternoon \nthrough Monday. Surface pressure gradient tightens a bit Sunday \nafternoon, which will lead to low-end breezy winds 15 to 25 mph.\n\nThe week ahead is a bit messy to forecast, given weak forcing \naloft and few day-to-day changes. Upper high drifts east over the \nSouthern Rockies Tuesday-Thursday, which will warm temperatures a \nfew degrees, putting us right near normal. Both global models \nresolve a developing low over Texas midweek, creating a weak Rex \nBlock pattern. There is plenty of moisture to our south, and we'll\nsee thunderstorm activity over Chihuahua through the week ahead. \nQuestion is whether or not that moisture can drift this far north.\nRight now, the answer is no and the forecast leans toward \ncontinued dry weather. Stronger mid-latitude flow flushes all this\nmoisture out by next weekend.",
